ASTRA — Chemical Transformation ResearchInitial investigation · 24 October 20261. Selected scientific direction: Partner-selective chemical transformationsI am pursuing a general problem in chemical manufacture:Can electrical or photochemical energy be used to control which two molecular fragments react with one another, rather than merely supplying enough energy for reactions to occur?The potential capability is a programmable chemical-coupling architecture that repeatedly joins selected molecular building blocks while suppressing unwanted combinations.This is consequential because constructing complex medicines, functional materials and new molecular structures often depends on controlling which bonds form, not simply making reactions faster.The opportunity search identified three relevant advances:A 2026 Nature Chemistry study demonstrated cross-coupling of radical precursors derived from carboxylic acids and boronic acids using alternating-polarity electrolysis. Nature ChemistryA 2026 Organic Letters study reported substrate-paired, light-activated complexes that enable selective coupling of distinct alkyl fragments. ACS PublicationsA 2025–2026 industrial flow-electrochemistry demonstration achieved kilogram-scale nickel-catalysed cross-electrophile coupling, including 120 hours of continuous operation. This establishes a serious manufacturing benchmark. ScienceDirectThese findings establish that selective coupling is experimentally accessible, but they do not establish a general programmable manufacturing system.The scientific opening is to determine when the physical association of intended reaction partners can become an independently controllable resource, beyond changing concentrations, catalysts or electrode potentials.2. First mathematical result: A selectivity limit for freely mixing radicalsConsider two reactive intermediates, A∙A^\bulletA∙ and B∙B^\bulletB∙. They can form the desired cross-coupled product ABABAB, or unwanted products AAAAAA and BBBBBB.