reactive states form, and which species can escape.Conceptual architecture only. No physical reactor with these combined capabilities has been demonstrated in this investigation.The envisioned cycle is:Two chemically distinguishable sites preferentially load the desired AAA and BBB precursors.Light or an electrode potential generates a reactive intermediate from the paired precursors.Fragmentation releases unwanted leaving groups while retaining the two carbon-centred reactive fragments close enough to couple.The product leaves, and the compartment returns to its loading state.The essential proposed advance is independent control over charge separation, radical retention and product release. A static cage that merely holds reactants together is not sufficient.How selective must the initial binding be?Under a simplified equilibrium model, let the correctly occupied ABABAB configuration have a free-energy advantage Δ\DeltaΔ over AAAAAA and BBBBBB. For equimolar reactants and equivalent binding-site degeneracies,qAB=11+exp⁡(−Δ/RT).q_{AB}=\frac{1}{1+\exp(-\Delta/RT)}.qAB​=1+exp(−Δ/RT)1​.At 298 K, the model predicts:Relative pairing preferenceCorrectly paired fraction0 kJ/mol50.0%10 kJ/mol98.26%12 kJ/mol99.22%15 kJ/mol99.76%Thus an advantage of approximately 11.4 kJ/mol would be enough for 99% correct occupancy in this particularly favourable model.This is not a universal energy requirement for synthesis. It assumes binding equilibrium, fixed stoichiometry and correctly counted configurations. Preferential light absorption may make the fraction of excited correct pairs quite different from their equilibrium occupancy.For a larger mixture, there is a more demanding combinatorial constraint. If one desired configuration competes with M−1M-1M−1 equally likely alternatives, selecting it with 99% probability requiresΔ≥RTln⁡[99(M−1)].\Delta \geq RT\ln[99(M-1)].Δ≥RTln[99(M−1)].For 100 alternatives, this becomes approximately 22.8 kJ/mol; for 10,000 alternatives, approximately 34.2 kJ/mol. A broadly programmable reactor would therefore need genuine molecular recognition or active rejection of incorrect configurations. Merely offering a cavity would not confer programmability.4. A failed mechanism: Greater confinement is not always betterThe decisive complication is that photochemical reactions often need both separation and confinement, at different stages.In a 2024 Nature Chemistry study, cage escape of photogenerated radical-ion pairs substantially governed product formation. Two photocatalysts exhibited cage-escape quantum yields of approximately 58% and 13% with one measured donor, even though both were capable of photoinduced electron transfer. This is evidence that producing a charge-separated state does not guarantee productive chemistry. Nature ChemistryThose experiments involved photocatalyst–donor radical-ion pairs, not the proposed alkyl-fragment pair. Their rates cannot simply be transferred here. Nevertheless, they expose a real physical obstacle to indiscriminate confinement.I represented the proposed chemistry as two consecutive competitions:Excited pair→kfreactive radical pair→kcAB,\text{Excited pair}
