\epsilon=\frac{k_e}{k_c},β=kf​kb​​,ϵ=kc​ke​​,we need(1+β)(1+ϵ)≤qYtarget.(1+\beta)(1+\epsilon)\leq\frac{q}{Y_{\rm target}}.(1+β)(1+ϵ)≤Ytarget​q​.For q=0.99765q=0.99765q=0.99765 and Ytarget=0.99Y_{\rm target}=0.99Ytarget​=0.99,(1+β)(1+ϵ)≤1.00773.(1+\beta)(1+\epsilon)\leq1.00773.(1+β)(1+ϵ)≤1.00773.Thus the combined penalties of reverse electron transfer and radical escape must be exceptionally small.I tested a conditional regime that independently changes the three controls:ParameterAssumed valueCorrect-pair probability qqq0.99765Productive fragmentation kfk_fkf​1010 s−110^{10}\ {\rm s}^{-1}1010 s−1Reverse electron transfer kbk_bkb​107 s−110^7\ {\rm s}^{-1}107 s−1Intrapair coupling kck_ckc​109 s−110^9\ {\rm s}^{-1}109 s−1Radical escape kek_eke​6×106 s−16\times10^6\ {\rm s}^{-1}6×106 s−1The calculated paired-product yield isYmodel=0.99071.\boxed{Y_{\rm model}=0.99071.}Ymodel​=0.99071.​99.07% is a synthetic model prediction, not an experimentally achieved yield or demonstrated material transformation.The requirement is revealing. Using a rough one-nanometre escape length and a freely diffusing radical relative diffusivity of 10−9 m2s−110^{-9}\ {\rm m^2s^{-1}}10−9 m2s−1, diffusion scaling gives an escape rate of order 6×109 s−16\times10^9\ {\rm s^{-1}}6×109 s−1. Reaching the assumed 6×106 s−16\times10^6\ {\rm s^{-1}}6×106 s−1 would require roughly a thousandfold reduction in escape at that scale.Yet productive coupling must remain near 109 s−110^9\ {\rm s^{-1}}109 s−1. If trapping the radicals also immobilizes them in the wrong geometry, the apparent advantage disappears.The most important unresolved physical operation is therefore preventing loss of partner identity without suppressing the bond-forming motion. Preorientation or transient coordination could, in principle, decouple those two requirements.6. Comparison against realized chemistryThe case for this architecture is substantially weaker when compared with the best current results rather than an uncontrolled statistical radical mixture.A particularly relevant 2026 Organic Letters study already uses substrate-paired electron donor–acceptor complexes for catalyst-free photochemical alkyl–alkyl coupling. Preassociation is therefore an existing chemical mechanism, not a novel invention here. pubs.acs.orgMoreover, a 2026 Green Chemistry system combines an iron-porphyrin cocatalyst with a recyclable carbon-nitride photocatalyst to sort radicals originating from several different precursor families, with reported gram-scale synthesis. That is a serious precedent for a reusable, multi-substrate transformation platform. Green Chemistry (RSC Publishing)The evidence is also strong in complementary regimes. Persistent-radical chemistry on Ag(111) achieved greater than 99% cross-coupling selectivity in a surface model system, and industrial nickel-electrocatalytic cross-coupling has reached kilogram scale and 120 hours of continuous operation, with an 87% in-process product yield reported for one 100 mmol run. Those are different substrates, mechanisms and performance metrics; neither should be compared numerically to my synthetic photon yield as if it were a head-to-head experiment. ACS Publications+1Photocatalytic molecular cages are also established: polymolybdate cages have produced up to 92% yield for certain oxidative cross-couplings, while chiral cages have controlled partner recognition and excited-state selectivity in photocycloadditions. ACS Publications+1Revised novelty assessment: Neither preassociation nor confinement nor selective radical generation is new. The potential distinct capability would be independently switching the chemical state that may escape while preserving specified reaction-partner identity, in a reusable manufacturing cycle. No experimental evidence presently establishes that capability.7. A decisive experiment that distinguishes the proposed principleA useful test must distinguish actual partner-memory retention from selective coupling produced by conventional radical kinetics.One route is an isotope crossover experiment. Prepare two chemically equivalent, differently labelled precursor pairs, A1B1A_1B_1A1​B1​ and A2B2A_2B_2A2​B2​, then mix them rapidly before activation.A mechanism that preserves the original pair should preferentially produce A1B1A_1B_1A1​B1​ and A2B2A_2B_2A2​B2​. A fully scrambled pool of radicals should also produce A1B2A_1B_2A1​B2​ and A2B1A_2B_1A2​B1​, even if its conventional radical chemistry remains highly selective for ABABAB rather than AAAAAA or BBBBBB.If a fraction mmm of product-forming events preserves original partners and the remainder randomizes completely, the mismatched-label fraction isfmismatch=1−m2.f_{\rm mismatch}=\frac{1-m}{2}.fmismatch​=21−m​.Hence fewer than 1% mismatched cross-products would imply greater than 98% partner memory under that mixture model, assuming isotope labels do not alter the kinetics and the two preformed populations were genuinely mixed before reaction.Varying the interval between mixing and illumination would then identify whether that memory persists or disappears through precursor exchange.The comparison must use the same substrates, solvent, illumination, reactant concentrations and analysis with and without the proposed host. Time-resolved absorption would separately test charge recombination, radical formation and escape. Product analysis would quantify conversion, cross-selectivity, isotope scrambling and decomposition.A chemically decisive outcome would be a host-dependent reduction in isotope crossover together with increased useful product formation, preserved throughput and repeated host turnover. Low crossover alone would be insufficient if conversion collapses.8. Energy and manufacturing constraintsEven a successful paired-radical gate is not an energy-free method of manufacturing.As an illustrative calculation, a 450 nm photon carries approximately 266 kJ/mol of photons. At the synthetic Y=0.99071Y=0.99071Y=0.99071, a one-photon-per-event process without chain amplification would need at least approximately 268 kJ/mol of product in absorbed photon energy. With an assumed 30% electrical-to-optical wall efficiency, that corresponds to roughly 894 kJ/mol of product in electricity for illumination alone, before pumping, precursor manufacture, separations and host regeneration.The assumed 450 nm absorption and 30% light-source efficiency are not measurements for this proposed chemistry. The energy preference for binding correct precursors is also not automatically an externally consumed work amount.The host must turn over. At an assumed active-host concentration of 10 mM, producing 1 mol/L/hour with the synthetic per-cycle yield would require approximately 101 completed productive cycles per host per hour: one loading-to-release cycle in roughly 36 seconds. If irreversible byproducts poison the host or product remains trapped, that rate is unattainable regardless of the radical-pair calculations.The actual precursor chemistry further matters. Alkylpyridinium and organoboron feedstocks carry chemical processing costs and produce leaving-group-derived wastes. No closed atom, electron or economic balance is yet justified for a general substrate pair.9. Result and next scientific uncertaintyThe supported outcome of this initial investigation is not a new manufacturing device. It is a more specific proposed physical control principle and two conditional mathematical findings:First, freely mixing radicals governed by equal symmetric pairwise coupling coefficients cannot exceed 50% cross-coupling selectivity simply by optimizing their concentration histories. This identifies a regime in which maintaining partner correlation can be genuinely valuable.Second, a model of confinement that simultaneously impedes productive fragmentation and radical escape has a finite optimum and can lose almost all productivity when confinement becomes excessive. Independent reaction-state control can escape that model limitation, but only when the required kinetic separation is physically achievable.The 99.07% synthetic construction establishes consistency of a proposed rate regime, not accessibility of those rates. The isotope crossover measurement offers a way to test the organising principle rather than infer it from product yield alone.The strongest next attack is therefore to find a chemically warranted reaction pair and regenerable molecular environment in which fragment separation is fast, intrapair coupling remains fast, and radical escape is slow—and to show that this combination improves useful manufacture against direct substrate-paired EDA chemistry and established radical-sorting catalysis.Astra's N769 result does not establish this chemistry's control law: the proposed photochemical intermediates are transient, effectively irreversible and outside its reversible elementary mass-action premises. N782's model-transfer lesson is directly relevant: independently favourable rates measured in incompatible environments cannot be assembled into a physical advantage.Current status: A constructive hypothesis, analytic selectivity and kinetic results, executable synthetic tests, and a falsifiable physical experiment. No acquired kinetic constants, demonstrated host cycle, matched experimental advantage or claim of historical novelty. The central discovery question remains open: whether molecular reaction-partner memory can be controlled independently of the chemical steps that generate and consume the reactive intermediates.
